  "executive_summary": "This paper proposes a universal quantitative measure for the organization of a system, grounded in the principle of least action from physics. The author argues that existing methods using information or entropy are insufficient and that the physical amount of action provides a more natural metric for system states. The core hypothesis is that the amount of organization is inversely proportional to the sum of actions of all elements in the system. The paper derives a mathematical expression for this organization measure and applies it to a simplified limiting case: a closed, two-dimensional system containing one free particle and one internal constraint. The model assumes no energy dissipation, constant mass and speed, and an isotropic medium. By applying Hamilton’s Principle, the author demonstrates that the actual trajectory of the element is the one with the least action. The study compares two states of the system where the constraint orientation changes the trajectory length. It is shown that the state with the shorter trajectory (lower action) possesses higher organization. Specifically, if one trajectory is twice as long as another, the corresponding state is calculated to be twice as well-organized. This result provides a method for comparing different levels of organization within the same system or between different systems. The paper concludes that this approach empowers natural and social sciences to quantify organization, facilitating the design of systems with higher perfection. Future parts of the series will extend the model to multiple elements and open systems, addressing time dependency and broader applicability.",
